# menu: skip the intro and reach the main menu.
#
# The intro-skip count is not fully deterministic (it depends on which
# intro frame is showing when a key lands), but DR_SHOTS's own default
# ("3:intro 9:menu") always fires before DR_KEYS starts and eats 9s of
# wall time first, so by the time this scenario's own Enter lands, the
# intro has almost always already run its course. One Enter with a
# generous wait reliably reached the main menu in repeated runs;
# a 2nd Enter measured as one Enter too many,
# overshooting into the "Start Racing" submenu instead. If a future
# scenario changes SHOTS away from the default, re-measure this.
#
# Earlier versions of this prefix used two Enters followed by an Esc, on
# the theory that Esc ("Previous menu") is a harmless no-op once already on
# the main menu: if the 2nd Enter only cleared the intro, Esc would do
# nothing; if it also opened "Start Racing", Esc would back out of it.
# Measurement showed otherwise: Esc
# on the main menu moves the highlighted item to the last one ("Exit to
# OS") instead of leaving it alone or backing out. Re-measured with
# two independent runs and confirmed identically both times, so Esc is no longer
# used to normalize onto the main menu anywhere in this scenario set. This
# does not affect Esc used *inside* a submenu (e.g. configure-effects.keys's
# trailing Esc, which is normal "Previous menu" behaviour and was not part
# of what those measurements covered).
#
# shot:menu is the known-state check used in place of Esc: look
# at the screenshot before trusting that a scenario built on this prefix
# landed on the main menu rather than still mid-intro or already one level
# into "Start Racing".
# Three Enters, as in results-orig.keys: the port now shows dr.exe's two
# publisher splashes (Apogee, then rmd.bmp) before its own intro, like the
# original, so the single Enter measured for the port before no longer
# reaches the main menu.
enter w3000
enter w3000
enter w3500
shot:menu
